{"reference":"1 Corinthians 9","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Paul defends a genuine right to material support before explaining that he chose not to use it in Corinth. His argument moves from ordinary work and Scripture to sacred service and the Lord’s direction that gospel proclaimers may live from the gospel. The point is not that spiritual service can be sold, nor that every worker must refuse support; Paul presents his own free-of-charge practice as a contextual renunciation intended to avoid hindering the gospel.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 9:1-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_009_3c1888fa14379d35c2c7e633"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Work and support","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The soldier, vineyard worker, shepherd, ox, plower, and thresher form a cumulative argument: those whose labor serves a task ordinarily share in its material provision. The agricultural images especially emphasize working in hope of receiving a share of the produce. They explain why Paul can speak of support as a right while still refusing to turn that right into unlimited compensation.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 9:7-10"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_009_bec4d62c6c2055b4fa1a4182"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Becoming all things to all people","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s adaptability is qualified by what he says about remaining accountable to Messiah. He describes adjusting his manner among Jews, people under the law, people outside the law, and the weak in order to reach them, not abandoning all boundaries. This distinction helps keep contextual communication separate from deception, impersonation, cultural erasure, exploitation, or concealed power.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 9:19-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_009_6a5db5b1433b6c85d71819e3"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Rights, restraint, and discipline","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter holds together rights, voluntary restraint, and purposeful discipline. Paul’s refusal to use support rights is his own practice, not a basis for coercive unpaid labor; likewise, his athletic language about training and bringing his body under control serves his concern for accountability after preaching, rather than authorizing harmful bodily demands on others.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 9:1-18","1 Corinthians 9:19-23","1 Corinthians 9:24-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_009_bba2e391bd007e0c6d81a05e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}